  "estimatedMinutes": 1,
  "statement": "A value of \\(x\\) satisfying the equation \\(x^2+b^2=(a-x)^2\\) is:",
  "answerChoices": [
    {
      "label": "A",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac{b^2+a^2}{2a}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "B",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac{b^2-a^2}{2a}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "C",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac{a^2-b^2}{2a}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "D",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac{a-b}{2}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "E",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac{a^2-b^2}{2}\\)"
    }
  ],
  "answer": {
    "letter": "c",
    "value": null
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.ahsme-1958-p9.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "Expanding the right side, \\[ x^2+b^2=a^2-2ax+x^2. \\] Cancelling \\(x^2\\) and solving gives \\[ \\begin{aligned} 2ax&=a^2-b^2,\\\\ x&=\\frac{a^2-b^2}{2a}. \\end{aligned} \\]\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is C.",
      "hints": [
        "Expand \\((a-x)^2\\)",
        "The \\(x^2\\)-terms cancel, leaving a linear equation in \\(x\\)"
      ],
      "images": []
    }
  ],
